Phase 2: OSI Model & TCP/IP Model
(The Most Important Networking Concept for Java Engineers)
If you master this phase, you'll understand:
•	How a browser reaches Google
•	How Spring Boot talks to PostgreSQL
•	How Keycloak validates tokens
•	Why APIs time out
•	Why firewalls block traffic
•	How HTTPS works at a high level
________________________________________
First: Why OSI Model Was Created
Imagine a courier company.
To deliver a package, different teams handle different jobs:
Packaging Team
Address Team
Transport Team
Delivery Team
Each team focuses on one responsibility.
Networking follows the same idea.
Instead of one giant protocol doing everything, networking is split into layers.
________________________________________
OSI Model
7 Application
6 Presentation
5 Session
4 Transport
3 Network
2 Data Link
1 Physical
Interview Formula:
User Data
↓
Application
↓
Transport
↓
Network
↓
Data Link
↓
Physical
Data travels downward when sending.
Data travels upward when receiving.
________________________________________
Easy Analogy
Suppose you're sending a letter.
Letter Content
↓
Envelope
↓
Address
↓
Truck
↓
Road
OSI works similarly.
Every layer adds its own information.
This process is called:
Encapsulation
________________________________________
Layer 1 - Physical Layer
Responsible for:
Moving bits
Examples:
Ethernet Cable
Fiber Optic
WiFi Radio Signals
It doesn't know:
•	Names
•	IP addresses
•	Websites
•	HTTP
It only knows:
101010101
________________________________________
